{"id":"monochrome-luxe","title":"Monochrome Luxe","url":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Monochrome_Luxe","rev":210920,"fetched":"2026-10-16","lead":[{"t":"p","c":[{"t":"b","c":["Monochrome Luxe"]}," is a semi-minimalist style of interior design that arose in the 80s and extended into the 90s. Originally a high-end style derived from high-tech architecture, over time it shed that association and became a style that was entirely appropriate for middle class design. Because it simplicity gives it a certain timelessness. It is still found in use occasionally."]}],"sections":[{"title":"Visuals","id":"visuals","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["A preference for smooth and glossy, but not heavily reflective materials: melamins, plastic, metal, glass (both clear and frosted), leather"]},{"c":["Matte or painted metals preferred over chrome"]},{"c":["Two-color interiors with accents. The palette usually of white and black, but sometimes another color replacing either of these"],"sub":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["In particular, avoidance of natural beige and brown tones. Material like varnished or untreated wood (wood floors occasionally show in later treatments) and raw linen are to be avoided"]}]}]},{"c":["Color blocking (e.g. in kitchen cabinets) over patterns"]},{"c":["Avoidance of large reflective surfaces (much more common in the art deco revivals of the period"]},{"c":["Lighting is mostly incandescent rather than colored, neons are rarely used as decor elements (in contrast with ",{"t":"a","c":["Laser Grid"],"id":"laser-grid"},")"]},{"c":["The light fixtures themselves are frequently articulated (desk-lamp style or spotlights), or they are postmodern in design, with heavy used of flat, cut glass for dramatic light refraction patterns"]},{"c":["Geometric furniture with simple, yet bold shapes"]},{"c":["Shapes are generally abstract. If present, patterns are simple and geometric: stripes, checkers..."]}]},{"t":"p","c":["Early elements that had faded out of the style by the early-mid nineties include:"]},{"t":"img","img":{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/0/05/Abitacolo_bed.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20241108230948","w":180,"h":240,"cap":[{"t":"br"},"Although dating to the early 70s, Bruno Munari's Abitacolo bed fits right into the 80s version of Monochrome Luxe",{"t":"br"}]}},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Steel trusses as architectural (e.g. to hang lights) or furniture elements"]},{"c":["Other industrial elements like embossed sheet steel"]}]}]},{"title":"Gallery","id":"gallery","blocks":[{"t":"gallery","items":[{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/0/02/Pic_Fix_1980s_Interior_Design.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20240526172700","w":185,"h":185,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Decoration and Design for the 80'"]}," (1983)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/1/14/Img_history_02.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20251024065041","w":185,"h":185,"cap":["Urban Core system by Toyo Kitchen (1984)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/e/e4/Original_4836209f09ddc17c94abf23b56ec7693.png/revision/latest?cb=20241108231447","w":185,"h":185,"cap":["Pierrot Cafe in Osaka, ",{"t":"i","c":["Cafes, Restaurants, Bars"]}," (1986)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/4/44/IKEA_Museum_-_sv-1987_0126.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20241108231323","w":185,"h":185,"cap":["Ikea catalogue, 1987"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/c/ce/IKEA_Museum_-_sv-1987_0139.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20241108231324","w":185,"h":185,"cap":["Ikea catalogue, 1987"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/1/16/IKEA_Museum_-_sv-1987_0157.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20241108231324","w":185,"h":185,"cap":["Ikea catalogue, 1987"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/d/dd/Tumblr_1a6c12006eaa103a77dfdec3c5aee450_3c6f8011_1280.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20251024065039","w":185,"h":185,"cap":["design by Celia Vogel and Mario Mulea, ",{"t":"i",